@node Fetch and set abstract
@subsection Fetch and set abstract

@findex tlon-fetch-and-set-abstract
The command @code{tlon-fetch-and-set-abstract} attempts to fetch an abstract for the Bib@TeX{} entry at point and set it in the @samp{abstract} field. It works in both @samp{bibtex-mode} and @samp{ebib-entry-mode}.

The command first checks whether proceeding is allowed based on @code{tlon-abstract-overwrite} (@ref{Abstract overwrite behavior}) and any existing abstract. It then tries three data sources in sequence: CrossRef (using the entry's DOI), Google Books (using its ISBN), and Zotra (using its URL or DOI). If an abstract is found, it is cleaned up (XML/HTML tags and extraneous prefixes are removed, and a trailing period is ensured) before being set. If no abstract is found, a message is displayed.

Programmatic callers may pass @samp{PRESERVE-EXISTING} to skip an existing abstract without prompting. The field is checked again after fetching, so an abstract added while a network request yields is preserved. The original buffer is retained, and a changed entry or Ebib database stops the write. Ordinary interactive overwrite confirmation is requested only once.

If Zotra reports that no translator returned metadata, the command reports that result and continues to the next source. The combined non-AI/AI command can then use its existing AI summarization step. Other Zotra errors, including authentication, network and programming failures, remain errors in both single-entry and batch calls. DOI-only entries also reach Zotra when CrossRef returns no abstract.

When invoked interactively from a Bib@TeX{} buffer with the Summarize @samp{batch} infix (@code{tlon-ai-batch-fun}) set, the command delegates to @code{tlon-batch-set-abstracts} with strategy @samp{non-ai} (@ref{Batch set abstracts}), which runs in the background without widening or saving the current buffer. Outside a Bib@TeX{} buffer, or when the infix is unset, the command still uses the legacy per-entry path driven by @code{tlon-ai-batch-continue}.

@node Batch set abstracts
@subsection Batch set abstracts

@findex tlon-batch-set-abstracts
The command @code{tlon-batch-set-abstracts} adds abstracts to entries in a Bib@TeX{} file that lack one. It takes two optional arguments: a @samp{FILE} to process (defaulting to the current Bib@TeX{} buffer, or prompting if none) and a @samp{STRATEGY}, one of the symbols @samp{non-ai}, @samp{ai}, or @samp{both} (the default).

The command returns immediately; all work happens in the background on a hidden copy of the file, so the user's Bib@TeX{} buffer is not widened, point is not moved, and the buffer is not saved in place. Progress is written to the log buffer @samp{*tlon-batch-abstract-log*} (pop it up with @code{tlon-batch-abstract-show-log}) and, for the AI pass, to the progress file defined by @code{tlon-batch-abstract-progress-file}.

The three strategies work as follows:

@itemize
@item
@samp{non-ai}: scans the file and tries CrossRef (via DOI), Google Books (via ISBN), and Zotra (via URL) for each entry without an abstract. Runs one entry per idle tick so Emacs stays responsive.
@item
@samp{ai}: queues every entry without an abstract that has an associated text file and sends each to the summarization model using the language-specific prompt from @code{tlon-ai-get-abstract-prompts}.
@item
@samp{both}: runs the non-AI pass first; any entry for which non-AI sources fail but that has an associated text file is queued for the AI pass.
@end itemize

If the user's Bib@TeX{} buffer for the target file has unsaved edits, the command errors out. If it is unmodified, it is quietly reverted when the batch completes so the new abstracts become visible.

@findex tlon-batch-abstract-resume
If the AI pass crashes or Emacs is restarted mid-run, @code{tlon-batch-abstract-resume} picks up from the saved progress file. @code{tlon-batch-abstract-stop} cancels a running batch while preserving the progress file for later resumption.

@vindex tlon-ai-batch-fun
The Summarize submenu exposes a @samp{batch} infix (see the corresponding section in the manual of @samp{tlon-ai}) whose value is a function symbol. When any of the summarize commands (@code{tlon-fetch-and-set-abstract}, @code{tlon-get-abstract-with-ai}, @code{tlon-get-abstract-with-or-without-ai}) is invoked interactively from a Bib@TeX{} buffer with this infix set, the command delegates to @code{tlon-batch-set-abstracts} with the matching strategy instead of processing only the entry at point. The @samp{s -o} (overwrite) infix still controls collision behaviour; set it to @samp{always} or @samp{never} for unattended runs, since @samp{ask} prompts mid-run.

@node Translate abstract
@subsection Translate abstract

@findex tlon-translate-abstract
The command @code{tlon-translate-abstract} translates the abstract of the Bib@TeX{} entry at point from its source language (determined by the @samp{langid} field) to a target language using DeepL@. If no target language is provided, you are prompted to select one. The translation is stored in the per-language JSON file at @samp{babel-refs/json/<code>/abstract-translations.json}. You can optionally supply a variable to accumulate translations in memory rather than writing to disk after each one.

@findex tlon-translate-abstracts
The function @code{tlon-translate-abstracts} extends this to multiple entries. It iterates through up to 2000 entries in the current Bib@TeX{} buffer, translating each one that does not already have a translation (unless the overwrite argument is set). When doing large batch translations, use the variable argument to avoid repeated file I/O@.

